1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,160
is an idiot.

2
00:00:01,160 --> 00:00:02,620
So anyone who continues to believe

3
00:00:02,620 --> 00:00:04,920
that minimum wage causes unemployment is an idiot.

4
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:07,560
So as it turns out, if you apply Bayes' rule,

5
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:10,120
you will see that neither of these extreme conclusions

6
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:11,440
makes much sense.

7
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:14,800
And rather, the sensible view is that you

8
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:19,640
should move your view in the direction of just leading

9
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:22,120
the standard view, that minimum wage causes unemployment.

10
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:23,840
But depending upon how good you think

11
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:26,360
econometric studies are, and how confident you

12
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:29,240
were to begin with, you may not actually

13
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:32,880
end up with a view that's very different from the standard one.

14
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:35,480
So here is the general application of Bayes' rule.

15
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:37,720
So Probability Minimum Wage Causes Unemployment,

16
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:40,200
given the Karl Kruger findings, is equal to the probability

17
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:41,900
that you'll get the Karl Kruger findings

18
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:44,340
if minimum wage does cause unemployment times

19
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:46,520
the probability it causes unemployment

20
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:49,240
all over probability Karl Kruger findings.

21
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:51,520
As you get those, if min wage does cause unemployment,

22
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:54,720
the probability it causes unemployment plus the chance

23
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:56,320
that you get the Karl Kruger findings,

24
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,400
if minimum wage does not cause unemployment,

25
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:01,480
All that times the probability does not cost any money.

26
00:01:01,480 --> 00:01:03,760
So I just picked some numbers, which

27
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:05,920
may seem plausible to me.

28
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:09,000
The numbers you pick may vary.

29
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Remember, if we're dealing with basic and prior probabilities,

30
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,880
then who says what they should be?

31
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:18,320
Yes, you say what they should be for yourself.

32
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:21,800
Someone could go and say your prior probabilities are silly.

33
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:24,040
But nevertheless, the formula does not

34
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:26,040
give any explanation as to where they come from.

35
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:27,600
You just have to get them.

36
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:29,880
You say, well, how do I get them if I don't have them?

37
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:32,240
Well, fortunately, you already do.

38
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:34,480
So there's no issue where you don't already

39
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:37,400
have some opinion, even if it is something

40
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:42,200
as obscure as, will this substance I've never

41
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:43,400
encountered kill me?

42
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:46,360
Well, most of us assume that things we never encountered

43
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:48,600
won't suddenly kill us.

44
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:51,000
Once in a while, it turns out to be wrong.

45
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,320
But usually, if there is a new food in a room,

46
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:56,120
you don't run away in terror saying,

47
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:58,200
I have no proof that won't kill me.

48
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:01,200
But instead, your presumption is, well, if it's food,

49
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:04,400
if it's around humans, probably it is not immediately fatal.

50
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:06,120
So I should not run.

51
00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:08,840
But you could, of course, be the paranoid person

52
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:11,400
who does assume the worst, a new food,

53
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:13,720
and thinks it will be fatal to ingest it.

54
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:17,920
Anyway, so the probabilities that I just picked out,

55
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:20,200
because they seem reasonable to me, is first of all,

56
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:22,360
probability that we get the current career findings

57
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:27,000
that the minimum wage does cause unemployment is 0.3.

58
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,680
So let's see.

59
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:30,920
So we get the set of another set of like this part.

60
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:32,520
We have this term here.

61
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:34,360
And actually, this term's the same here.

62
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:36,800
So I assign those equal to 0.3.

63
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:38,960
And again, the idea is that if the minimum wage does

64
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:41,280
cause unemployment, at least you would

65
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:43,960
expect it's more likely that you would get a result consistent

66
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:46,000
with that than not.

67
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:47,120
Seems reasonable.

68
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:49,360
So if the minimum wage really does cause unemployment,

69
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:51,920
and you do a high-quality empirical study,

70
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:54,040
you would think that it is more likely you will confirm

71
00:02:54,040 --> 00:02:56,040
the truth than that you wouldn't.

72
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:57,640
You could imagine a perverse world

73
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:03,200
where whatever is true turns out to be disproven experimentally.

74
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:05,960
Like your experiments are controlled by Satan

75
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:08,000
or something, so he's always there to deceive you.

76
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,840
Whatever the real result is, you're like, ah, I don't care.

77
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:12,880
I'm going to push the results to make it the opposite of whatever

78
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:14,000
it seems to be.

79
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,420
I'll plant the world with fossils

80
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:17,600
to spread the lie about Musa or whatever.

81
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:18,960
All right, fine.

82
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:23,400
Normally, you would not think that, at least I wouldn't.

83
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:25,760
And then similarly, we have probability

84
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:27,880
that you get the Karl Kruger findings if it does not

85
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:29,240
cause unemployment.

86
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:33,040
So this term over here, let's say that's equal to 0.8.

87
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:35,560
Namely, that if the minimum wage doesn't cause unemployment,

88
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:37,400
then you would expect a good empirical study

89
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:39,600
to find that it doesn't cause unemployment.

90
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:40,100
Yes?

91
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:42,480
I was curious why I was doing that up to 1.

92
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:46,280
So here's the key thing is that these probabilities don't

93
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:49,800
have to because they're not complementarily probabilities.

94
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:51,560
So think about it this way.

95
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:54,920
Could be that, so for example, it

96
00:03:54,920 --> 00:03:57,080
might be that there's an 80% chance

97
00:03:57,080 --> 00:03:59,520
you get the Karl Kruger findings no matter what because the

98
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:01,920
methods are not informative.

99
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:06,360
And say, you might think it's just a correct process where

100
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:09,440
whatever the truth is, 80% chance

101
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:11,400
that they'll just say the minimum wage doesn't

102
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:13,240
cause unemployment.

103
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:16,240
So in general, there's no need for the probability

104
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:19,240
of A given B plus the probability of A given not B

105
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:20,880
to add up to 1.

106
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:24,760
So you think about the probability that you will.

107
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:27,600
Let's see, what would be a good example here that makes sense?

108
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:31,240
So the probability that you will go past the exam

109
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:34,880
if you drank tea the night before, so that might be 0.9.

110
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:36,280
And the probability that you will

111
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:38,500
pass the exam if you didn't drink tea the night before

112
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:39,800
might also be 0.9.

113
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:41,120
And that sums to 1.8.

114
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:42,200
And how can that be?

115
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:45,800
Because there's no reason for those two things to sum to 1.

116
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:46,300
Make sense?

117
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:48,680
But it's a very good question.

118
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:51,160
And now, there is a part that does have to sum to 1.

119
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:54,280
So probability of causes plus probability of not causes,

120
00:04:54,280 --> 00:04:57,320
that does have to sum to 1.

121
00:04:57,320 --> 00:05:01,920
And then, I'll let us go and put in some probabilities that

122
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:03,440
make sense to me.

123
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:05,840
So I would just say, before there's any empirical studies,

124
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:07,800
I just say 99% certain that the minimum wage

125
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:10,000
causes unemployment.

126
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,400
Because that intro textbook and how awesome it is.

127
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:17,320
And also, how someone said that a minimum price for asparagus

128
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:19,120
wouldn't cost surplus asparagus.

129
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:20,160
You'd be like, what?

130
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:20,840
Come on.

131
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:21,640
No way.

132
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:23,800
All right, and like, if someone just

133
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:27,720
started making up examples of products and saying, hey,

134
00:05:27,720 --> 00:05:29,640
would that one cause a surplus?

135
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:30,720
Yes, that one would.

136
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:31,720
And that one.

137
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:34,040
How about a minimum price for markers?

138
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:36,120
Totally, that would cause a surplus of markers.

139
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:37,080
And paper.

140
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:40,760
Suddenly, all of these other things, then why not labor?

141
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:41,600
I don't know.

142
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:42,840
Doesn't seem like it would.

143
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:44,960
But then, of course, the probability doesn't.

144
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:47,560
That does have to equal 1 minus the probability it does.

145
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:49,480
So that will be 0.01.

146
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:51,760
And then, we can plug all these in.

147
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:57,760
So we get 0.3 times 0.99 over 0.3 times 0.99 plus 0.8 times

148
00:05:57,760 --> 00:05:58,880
0.01.

149
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:00,120
And what do we come up with?

150
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:03,280
Well, we have the anodized bias, which is 97.4%

151
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:05,040
the minimum wage causes unemployment,

152
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:08,120
despite the Karger findings.

153
00:06:08,120 --> 00:06:11,680
And again, how can we come up with so much confidence

154
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:14,520
in the orthodox result, given that the actual empirical study

155
00:06:14,520 --> 00:06:15,520
said something else?

156
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:16,560
Well, there's two parts.

157
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:19,520
First of all, how confident were we to begin with?

158
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:22,560
And then secondly, how good are the methods?

159
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:24,520
So if the methods are not actually that great,

160
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:28,240
even at their best, and you just sit around thinking

161
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:30,720
about all the ways things could be messed up,

162
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:33,480
there's endless ways things could be messed up.

163
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:36,360
So you probably heard about the replication crisis

164
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:37,880
in psychology.

165
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:40,080
So there's a bunch of ways that things seem messed up

166
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:41,080
in psychology.

167
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:44,520
So economics has not been doing as badly.

168
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:46,960
But still, if you have actually been

169
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:48,680
involved in any empirical work, you're like, wow,

170
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:49,600
things could be really messed up.

171
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:51,000
Or if you ever tried to replicate anyone,

172
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,340
did anyone here ever have a replication project where you

173
00:06:53,340 --> 00:06:56,200
just tried to go and match what somebody else did?

174
00:06:56,200 --> 00:06:58,840
And you'll find problems there.

175
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:02,200
And then, of course, there are two senses of replication.

176
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:04,280
So you may have heard about John Lott's libel suit

177
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:05,240
against Steve Levitt.

178
00:07:05,240 --> 00:07:06,120
I think it was Steve.

179
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:07,240
Yeah, I think it was Steve.

180
00:07:07,240 --> 00:07:07,740
What was it?

181
00:07:07,740 --> 00:07:09,240
It was the Levitt co-author.

182
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:11,720
Anyway, so I want to be careful here.

183
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:13,300
When you're talking about libel cases,

184
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:14,800
you better describe them accurately.

185
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:20,200
But in any case, the lawsuit involved

186
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:22,080
the definition of replication.

187
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:25,640
So someone, Lott sued someone who

188
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:27,600
said that his results did not replicate.

189
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:29,440
And Lott said they totally replicated.

190
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:32,040
Because to replicate means to get the same result if you

191
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:33,720
do exactly the same thing.

192
00:07:33,720 --> 00:07:35,480
And the other guy said, no, by replicate,

193
00:07:35,480 --> 00:07:38,240
I meant that if you just do another empirical study,

194
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:40,760
you'd get a different result.

195
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:42,120
And these are two senses.

196
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:44,240
But anyway, which sense is actually?

197
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:49,320
So there's the low bar of getting the very same result

198
00:07:49,320 --> 00:07:50,760
if you do exactly the same thing.

199
00:07:50,760 --> 00:07:52,480
But that's not all that impressive, is it?

200
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:52,980
Right?

201
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:55,000
Because you might then say, yeah, OK,

202
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,160
if I do it exactly the way, I get exactly the same result

203
00:07:57,160 --> 00:07:57,660
you did.

204
00:07:57,660 --> 00:08:00,080
You passed the very basic test.

205
00:08:00,080 --> 00:08:03,320
But my real criticism is your methods were crummy.

206
00:08:03,320 --> 00:08:04,980
And that would be a stronger criticism.

207
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:08,560
And that's something that you can always apply.

208
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:10,240
So what about for an experiment?

209
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:12,160
Even for an experiment, you can find problems

210
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:13,120
with an experiment.

211
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:14,440
So in experimental fields, people

212
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:15,320
would criticize, well, the experiment

213
00:08:15,320 --> 00:08:16,760
wasn't really done very well.

214
00:08:16,760 --> 00:08:18,760
Or you were sort of leading the witness.

215
00:08:18,760 --> 00:08:20,560
Or it doesn't mean what you think it means.

216
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:23,840
So there's always problems.

217
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:26,840
All right, so questions about this

218
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:29,680
as a general approach to scientific inference?

219
00:08:29,680 --> 00:08:30,180
Yes?

220
00:08:30,180 --> 00:08:38,160
I mean, I guess in a sense, this isn't really

221
00:08:38,160 --> 00:08:40,560
how we work as humans, right?

222
00:08:40,560 --> 00:08:41,920
Ah, great question.

223
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:44,880
So we're going to talk about that a lot after the midterm,

224
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:47,400
because there's a lot of scientific work,

225
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:49,400
especially in psychology, but also in economics,

226
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:52,040
about whether people really do this in practice

227
00:08:52,040 --> 00:08:54,320
or act as if they do.

228
00:08:54,320 --> 00:08:55,760
We know they don't really do this,

229
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:58,880
because if I gave everyone here a test with a bunch of problems

230
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:01,680
like this, I bet a lot of people would blow a lot of the problems.

231
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:04,240
And you guys have way more training and familiarity

232
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:06,120
with this kind of stuff than almost any human.

233
00:09:06,120 --> 00:09:08,880
So if you guys can't do it perfectly,

234
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:11,040
well, the odds of my mom's doing it.

235
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:12,360
That seems really unlikely.

236
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:13,880
But then there is the question, well,

237
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:15,680
maybe people act as if they're doing this,

238
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:17,880
at least when they're dealing with familiar problems,

239
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:18,640
things like this.

240
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:19,920
So we will come back to that.

241
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:21,680
But that is a key issue.

242
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:24,080
Now, the main thing you can say is, at minimum,

243
00:09:24,080 --> 00:09:25,920
this is what we call a normative principle.

244
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:27,760
This is the way you should be updating your beliefs.

245
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:30,640
This is the way a reasonable person updates their beliefs.

246
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:31,640
Doesn't answer the question of where

247
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:33,140
does a reasonable person get beliefs

248
00:09:33,140 --> 00:09:36,400
in the first place, which is probably also very important.

249
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:38,400
In fact, it's certainly also very important

250
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:40,840
to where you start.

251
00:09:40,840 --> 00:09:43,120
I don't know if Don Boudreau did this explicitly

252
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:44,800
and dumbed it down for a debate, but he

253
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:47,280
made this argument in the debate about the minimum wage,

254
00:09:47,280 --> 00:09:51,480
basically saying that we have 99% confidence in the theory.

255
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:53,320
And yes, there is countervailing evidence,

256
00:09:53,320 --> 00:09:55,520
but it's just not strong enough to overcome the theory.

257
00:09:55,520 --> 00:09:56,020
Right.

258
00:09:56,020 --> 00:09:57,480
And also, the end view.

259
00:09:57,480 --> 00:09:59,520
Karl Kruger's study is probably the most famous

260
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:00,640
in the history of minimum wage research.

261
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:01,640
There's a lot of other research.

262
00:10:01,640 --> 00:10:02,280
More recent.

263
00:10:02,280 --> 00:10:04,040
So we can sort of the way that Karl Kruger framed it as well,

