00:12:20,280 --> 00:12:23,640
OK, you get all them, and the Americans get the aliens.

327
00:12:23,640 --> 00:12:27,720
But it does start to sound like a pretty crazy story.

328
00:12:27,720 --> 00:12:30,720
I have no clear disproof that there's not

329
00:12:30,720 --> 00:12:32,560
some kind of property rights assigned

330
00:12:32,560 --> 00:12:35,040
to which humans get abducted by which species of alien.

331
00:12:35,040 --> 00:12:39,680
But it does start to sound like a pretty silly theory.

332
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:42,160
Then when you read about shocking disaster stories

333
00:12:42,160 --> 00:12:45,000
in the New York Times, you almost instantly believe them,

334
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,600
no matter how weird they are.

335
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:48,240
Whereas if it's the weekly world news,

336
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:51,160
anyone ever seen this newspaper?

337
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:55,160
It's the original and most awesome of all fake news.

338
00:12:55,160 --> 00:12:56,360
Bat Boy.

339
00:12:56,360 --> 00:12:57,840
And well, I think my favorite one

340
00:12:57,840 --> 00:12:59,440
would be Bat Boy Elected Pope.

341
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:04,680
All right, so the New York Times did Bat Boy's Elected Pope.

342
00:13:04,680 --> 00:13:05,440
I might check.

343
00:13:05,440 --> 00:13:07,020
It was just really the New York Times.

344
00:13:07,020 --> 00:13:09,600
But I don't think they would say that.

345
00:13:09,600 --> 00:13:12,760
Bat Boy Elected Pope.

346
00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:13,520
I remember so long.

347
00:13:13,520 --> 00:13:16,080
September 11, 2001, I told Robin Hanson

348
00:13:16,080 --> 00:13:18,280
that the World Trade Center has been destroyed.

349
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:21,640
And he looked at a watch to see if it was April 1.

350
00:13:21,640 --> 00:13:23,720
It's like, hmm, it's not April 1.

351
00:13:23,720 --> 00:13:26,440
So maybe I should believe this.

352
00:13:26,440 --> 00:13:28,080
And then similarly, also, we generally

353
00:13:28,080 --> 00:13:29,960
don't change our minds about the minimum wage

354
00:13:29,960 --> 00:13:32,120
when astronomers discover a new galaxy,

355
00:13:32,120 --> 00:13:35,000
because our view about the effects of minimum wage

356
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,840
are the same whether or not that galaxy exists.

357
00:13:37,840 --> 00:13:40,160
So view of the minimum wage will raise unemployment given

358
00:13:40,160 --> 00:13:42,680
if the galaxy does exist is equal to the probability

359
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:45,560
the minimum wage will raise unemployment if that galaxy

360
00:13:45,560 --> 00:13:46,240
does not exist.

361
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:49,040
So we don't change our minds when something irrelevant pops

362
00:13:49,040 --> 00:13:49,540
up.

363
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:57,480
Now, we update our beliefs in at least the general ballpark

364
00:13:57,480 --> 00:13:59,560
of this equation, as fortunate, because we're

365
00:13:59,560 --> 00:14:02,160
going to see is that game theory and information economics all

366
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:05,760
rely heavily upon this equation.

367
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:06,960
Now, after the midterm, we're going

368
00:14:06,960 --> 00:14:08,640
to look at a lot of evidence that the equation's not

369
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:10,440
perfect and there's a bunch of problems.

370
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:13,440
And we're also going to talk a bit about people's priors

371
00:14:13,440 --> 00:14:15,760
and how weird people's priors can be.

372
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:17,560
So of course, that's one where if you just

