00:14:17,560 --> 00:14:20,520
share the weird priors, then you say it's not weird.

374
00:14:20,520 --> 00:14:22,080
It's weird that you doubt it.

375
00:14:22,080 --> 00:14:24,320
It's weird that you don't believe the alien abduction

376
00:14:24,320 --> 00:14:25,520
reports.

377
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:28,640
So I don't have any definitive answer there

378
00:14:28,640 --> 00:14:31,400
other than the just incredulous look of, really?

379
00:14:31,400 --> 00:14:34,680
You really believe the aliens?

380
00:14:34,680 --> 00:14:36,800
Don't think of anything that you probably

381
00:14:36,800 --> 00:14:38,440
would have thought of before yourself.

382
00:14:38,440 --> 00:14:39,920
But anyway.

383
00:14:39,920 --> 00:14:41,360
Now, one last application.

384
00:14:41,360 --> 00:14:43,560
Suppose that you think you witness a zero probability

385
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:44,120
event.

386
00:14:44,120 --> 00:14:47,920
What should you conclude according to Bayes' rule?

387
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:49,760
It's still a zero probability event.

388
00:14:49,760 --> 00:14:50,320
What's that?

389
00:14:50,320 --> 00:14:52,480
It's still a zero probability event.

390
00:14:52,480 --> 00:14:54,320
Well, think about this.

391
00:14:54,320 --> 00:14:57,440
Is there any chance at all that you have wrongly

392
00:14:57,440 --> 00:14:59,280
thought that you wrongly concluded something

393
00:14:59,280 --> 00:15:01,320
was a zero probability event?

394
00:15:01,320 --> 00:15:03,040
There's even a one in a million chance

395
00:15:03,040 --> 00:15:06,120
that you're wrong about that and you think you see one.

396
00:15:06,120 --> 00:15:08,720
You say, oh my god, well, maybe I

397
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:10,840
was wrong to think it was a zero probability event.

398
00:15:10,840 --> 00:15:12,800
If you work through the math, then you realize,

399
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:14,600
wow, given that I would update it all,

400
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:19,200
given that it occurs, it means that I was wrong

401
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:20,400
to assign a zero probability.

402
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:23,240
Because remember, if there's a 99% chance that something

403
00:15:23,240 --> 00:15:26,400
is a zero probability, does it have a zero probability?

404
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:28,040
No, it doesn't have a zero probability.

405
00:15:28,040 --> 00:15:30,080
It has a 99% chance that it's zero.

406
00:15:30,080 --> 00:15:33,080
Because if there's a 1% chance that it's not zero,

407
00:15:33,080 --> 00:15:34,760
and it's 1% chance that it's not zero,

408
00:15:34,760 --> 00:15:39,920
what is 0.99 times 0 plus 0.01 times its other probability?

409
00:15:39,920 --> 00:15:41,600
Must be, what is that?

410
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:42,600
Let's put it this way.

411
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:43,840
We got it.

412
00:15:43,840 --> 00:15:47,320
Zero, and then less than, equal to, or greater than,

413
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:51,800
less than, or equal to 0.99 times 0, times 0,

414
00:15:51,800 --> 00:15:54,960
plus 0.01 times some other probability.

415
00:15:54,960 --> 00:16:00,600
So what do we know just based upon the nature of probability?

416
00:16:00,600 --> 00:16:04,080
Is zero less than, equal to, or greater than, less than,

417
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:06,840
greater than, equal to, or less than that?

418
00:16:06,840 --> 00:16:07,840
Yes, it must be.

419
00:16:07,840 --> 00:16:09,960
So it must be less than that, strictly,

420
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:12,520
because if that is even one in a million,

421
00:16:12,520 --> 00:16:14,840
then that thing is greater than zero.

422
00:16:14,840 --> 00:16:17,320
And then you have no business assigning a zero probability

423
00:16:17,320 --> 00:16:18,280
to it.

424
00:16:18,280 --> 00:16:20,360
Now, after the midterm, we'll talk

425
00:16:20,360 --> 00:16:24,560
about how human beings have a weird tendency

426
00:16:24,560 --> 00:16:28,440
to actually assign zero probabilities in the real world.

427
00:16:28,440 --> 00:16:30,840
And then, in fact, if you go and make

428
00:16:30,840 --> 00:16:34,200
a graph of probabilities people assign,

429
00:16:34,200 --> 00:16:36,800
and then the actual observed frequencies,

430
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:39,320
when people assign a 0% chance to something,

431
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:43,280
generally it has about a 20% chance of actually happening.

432
00:16:43,280 --> 00:16:45,160
And similarly, if they assign 100% chance,

433
00:16:45,160 --> 00:16:48,240
that means that that's like 80.

434
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:51,760
So there does seem to be a human tendency

435
00:16:51,760 --> 00:16:57,000
to be overly free with the extreme probabilities, which

436
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:00,640
the simple interpretation is human beings are just wrong.

437
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:02,920
There's also just the one that human beings speak loosely

438
00:17:02,920 --> 00:17:06,600
and enjoy hyperbole, and they're not serious.

439
00:17:06,600 --> 00:17:08,400
So when people say it's certain, they

440
00:17:08,400 --> 00:17:10,280
don't really mean it's certain.

441
00:17:10,280 --> 00:17:13,960
So we'll talk about that more after the midterm.

